WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.832
Choosing between Type I, Type II, or a staged path is a practical
decision.

2
00:00:05.832 --> 00:00:09.580
There is no single correct path for every organization.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:15.829
The choice depends on customer expectations, program maturity, sales
needs, risk, budget, timeline, auditor readiness,

4
00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:19.994
and the organization's ability to operate controls consistently over time.

5
00:00:19.994 --> 00:00:24.159
A first-time SOC 2 program may start with Type I.

6
00:00:24.159 --> 00:00:30.408
This can be useful when the organization needs to validate control design,
build customer confidence,

7
00:00:30.408 --> 00:00:36.239
and create a structured milestone before committing to a full Type II
review period.

8
00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:42.487
Type I can help the team learn the audit process, refine scope, clarify
control owners,

9
00:00:42.487 --> 00:00:45.820
and identify gaps before the evidence burden increases.

10
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:50.818
Type II is often more valuable for customers who want ongoing assurance.

11
00:00:50.818 --> 00:00:58.316
Enterprise customers, regulated customers, or mature vendor risk teams may
prefer or require Type II because it demonstrates

12
00:00:58.316 --> 00:01:00.399
that controls operated over time.

13
00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:07.896
A Type II report can be stronger evidence that the organization has moved
beyond policy design into operational

14
00:01:07.896 --> 00:01:09.696
discipline.

15
00:01:09.696 --> 00:01:13.445
Some organizations use Type I as a stepping stone.

16
00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:20.527
They complete Type I to confirm design and point-in-time readiness, then
begin the Type II review period.

17
00:01:20.527 --> 00:01:28.024
This can be a reasonable path when the organization is building its
program while also responding to customer

18
00:01:28.024 --> 00:01:29.824
assurance needs.

19
00:01:29.824 --> 00:01:37.322
The key is to avoid treating Type I as the permanent end state if
customers or risks require

20
00:01:37.322 --> 00:01:39.122
operating effectiveness evidence.

21
00:01:39.122 --> 00:01:46.620
Other organizations may go directly to Type II if their controls are
already mature, evidence workflows are stable,

22
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.703
and customer expectations are clear.

23
00:01:48.703 --> 00:01:56.200
This path can work when the organization has operated controls for long
enough and can produce evidence consistently.

24
00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:03.281
It can be risky if control ownership is unclear or evidence collection is
still manual and inconsistent.

25
00:02:03.281 --> 00:02:05.781
The decision should be made honestly.

26
00:02:05.781 --> 00:02:12.445
Do not choose Type II only because it sounds better if the program cannot
support it.

27
00:02:12.445 --> 00:02:17.860
Do not choose Type I while implying that it proves months of operation.

28
00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:24.942
Define scope, assess readiness, talk with qualified advisors and auditors
at an educational level, understand customer requirements,

29
00:02:24.942 --> 00:02:24.957
and choose the path that matches reality.
